@page "/privacy"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]
@attribute [ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]
@layout AboutLayout
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Options
@using ShelfAware.Llm
@using ShelfAware.Web.Auth
@inject IOptions<LlmOptions> Llm
@inject IOptions<EmailOptions> Email
@inject IOptions<AdminOptions> Admin
@inject IOptions<DemoOptions> Demo
@inject IOptions<AuthOptions> Auth

@* The data-handling statement: what this deployment stores, what leaves it, who can see what, and how
   to get it out. Public and static (no circuit) like /about, on the same layout, so it can be read before
   an account exists — Register links here. It describes the DEPLOYMENT it runs on, read from the same
   options the code reads, so a self-hosted box and the managed demo each get the true version rather
   than one paragraph that is wrong for the other. No legal boilerplate: a plain account of what the code
   does, which is the only kind a reader can check against the source. *@

<PageTitle>Privacy &amp; data — Reginald</PageTitle>

<article class="about">
    <section class="about-hero">
        <h1>Privacy &amp; data</h1>
        <p class="about-tagline">What this app keeps, what it sends out, and how you take it back.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-story">
        <h2>What is stored on this server</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Your account:</strong> the email you signed up with, a hashed password, and when you
                first and last signed in.</li>
            <li><strong>Your household's pantry:</strong> products, purchases, out/low signals, counts,
                expiration labels, recipes, grocery list, meal plans, tags, settings — and the receipts you
                upload, as line items <em>and</em> as the original images, so a read can be checked or
                retried. A receipt that could not be read is kept too, for the same reason.</li>
            <li><strong>Bug reports you file,</strong> with the page snapshot you chose to include.</li>
            <li><strong>The About page's "notify me" list:</strong> the plan you clicked, when, and, only if you
                typed one, your email address — no account needed, and nothing else about you.</li>
            <li><strong>Operator records:</strong> an error log (which can incidentally contain a file path or
                a household id from a failing request), per-household AI usage counts, and the credit ledger
                where billing is enabled.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Nothing is kept for analytics or advertising. There is no tracking script. The only cookies are
            the sign-in cookie, the one that protects forms from forgery, and a five-second status message
            between sign-in pages. Your theme and tour position live in your own browser.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-story">
        <h2>What leaves this server</h2>
        @if (Llm.Value.IsManaged)
        {
            <p>
                The AI features run on <strong>@Llm.Value.Provider</strong>, on a key whoever runs this
                deployment provides. Each request sends only what that feature needs:
            </p>
        }
        else
        {
            <p>
                The AI features run on the provider <strong>you</strong> pick in Settings, on a key that stays
                in your browser and rides each request to this server just long enough to make the call —
                it is never written to a database or a log. Each request sends only what that feature needs:
            </p>
        }
        <ul>
            <li>reading a receipt sends the receipt image and the names of products you already have;</li>
            <li>counting a shelf sends the shelf photos and your product names (the photos are not kept);</li>
            <li>the assistant sends what you typed or said, with your product list and tags for context;</li>
            <li>recipe ideas, imports and meal plans send the recipe text or photo and what's on hand.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            A receipt can carry a merchant, a date, a card's last digits, or a delivery address — so this is
            the one place personal data goes to a third party, and it is the reason the feature exists.
            The provider's own retention terms apply to what it receives.
        </p>
        <p>
            If voice features are switched on here, the audio you record goes to whichever speech engine
            this deployment is configured with — a model running on this server, or a cloud service — and
            the spoken replies are cached as audio files in your household's own folder.
        </p>
        <p>Nothing else is sent anywhere. The code is public, so this is checkable.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-story">
        <h2>Who can see what</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Your household</strong> shares one pantry: everyone you invite sees everything in it,
                and anyone in it can remove any member or delete all its data. Only share an invite code with
                people you'd share the kitchen with.</li>
            <li><strong>Other households</strong> cannot see yours. Every table is filtered by household on
                read and refused on write.</li>
            @if (Admin.Value.Configured)
            {
                <li><strong>The person running this server</strong> has an admin page showing account emails, sign-in
                    times and counts, who is online now, household names and their members, the "notify me"
                    emails, the bug reports you file (with any snapshot you included), the error log, a
                    recent-activity list (product and merchant names) and aggregate AI spend. It never shows
                    an API key.</li>
            }
            else
            {
                <li><strong>The person running this server</strong> can read its databases directly, as with
                    any self-hosted software. No admin page is configured here.</li>
            }
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="about-story">
        <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
        <p>
            Until you delete it. Receipt images are not aged out. Where backups are configured, a nightly
            copy of the databases and images is kept for a short fixed window and then overwritten, so
            deleted data can persist in a backup for that window.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="about-story">
        <h2>Taking it back</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Export:</strong> Settings → Your data → <em>Download my data</em> gives you every
                pantry record as JSON plus your receipt images, in a zip.</li>
            <li><strong>Delete:</strong> Settings → <em>Delete all my data</em> removes the household's pantry,
                bug reports and settings in one transaction, then its receipt images, cached audio and recipe
                images from disk and its API tokens.</li>
            <li><strong>What delete does not reach:</strong> your sign-in itself, the household record, the
                operator's usage and credit records, and an email left on the "notify me" list. There is no self-service account deletion yet — ask
                whoever runs this server to remove the account@(Email.Value.IsConfigured ? ", by replying to any email it has sent you" : "").</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    @if (Demo.Value.IsSharedDemo)
    {
        <section class="about-story">
            <h2>This is a shared demo box</h2>
            <p>
                Its AI features have daily limits@(Auth.Value.EffectiveDailyAccountCreationLimit is not null
                    ? ", and it takes a limited number of new accounts a day" : ""). The app tells you when one
                is hit. Treat it as a
                place to try things, not as the only copy of anything you care about; see
                <a href="https://github.com/Jcurran-Repo/ShelfAware/blob/master/SUPPORT.md">SUPPORT</a> for
                what to expect and how to report a problem.
            </p>
        </section>
    }

    <section class="about-story">
        <p class="muted">
            Questions about any of this: open an issue on
            <a href="https://github.com/Jcurran-Repo/ShelfAware">GitHub</a>, or use the bug-report link in
            the app's footer once signed in.
        </p>
    </section>
</article>
